This finding and the earlier "Preserve migrated model" finding (resolved in 2aa870a) are both correct for different scenarios — which is why the two suggestions contradict each other:

- `String::new()` is correct when configuring a *new* provider (B) while another (A) is active — B has no model yet
- `get_goose_model()` is correct when re-authenticating the *active* provider — it preserves the model that migration just created

The root cause is a read-write skew: `get_provider_entry()` reads through `load()` which skips provider migration, while `set_provider_entry()` writes through `load_write_config()` which runs migration. The `else` branch fires because the read sees pre-migration state (no `providers:` block), but the write happens post-migration, so whatever model we construct overwrites what migration created.

Fixed by branching on whether the provider being configured is the active provider:

```rust
let model = if goose::config::get_active_provider(config).as_deref()
    == Some(provider_name.as_str())
{
    config.get_goose_model().unwrap_or_default()
} else {
    String::new()
};
```

Same provider → preserves active model (matches migration output). Different provider → empty model (user picks one after auth). Compiles clean and passes `cargo fmt --check`.
